5:[["$","$L10","3.8",{"topic":{"id":"3.8","title":"Device and Computer Forensics","minutes":15,"summary":"Examine storage, operating-system artifacts, applications, and memory as parts of one system rather than isolated files.","sections":[{"title":"Storage and filesystem context","body":["A disk image can preserve partitions, filesystem metadata, allocated files, and unallocated space. NTFS, APFS, ext4, and other filesystems record different artifacts and timestamp semantics. A modified time may change because of synchronization, metadata updates, or software behavior. Record the filesystem and tool interpretation before building a timeline.","On Windows, registry hives, event logs, prefetch, shortcuts, jump lists, and shellbags can help reconstruct program execution, file access, and device connections. Their presence and detail depend on configuration and version. On Linux and macOS, examine the corresponding system logs, application databases, and configuration records rather than applying Windows assumptions."]},{"title":"Memory and running state","body":["RAM may contain processes, network connections, loaded modules, encryption keys, and unsaved content. Acquisition can alter the system and require trusted tools and elevated privileges. Capture only within authority and with a clear purpose. Record tool-induced changes, acquisition failures, and the system state. A memory image can contain highly sensitive material unrelated to the case.","Analyze process relationships, command lines, modules, and network artifacts in context. A legitimate process name can be imitated, and a signed binary can be misused. Memory findings can corroborate disk artifacts, but volatile observations are tied to the capture time and should not be presented as a complete history."]},{"title":"Attribution and anti-forensics","body":["A user-account artifact does not prove which human was present. Consider shared credentials, remote sessions, scheduled tasks, synchronization, and malware. Correlate logon events, application activity, physical access, and independent communications. Explain why the combined evidence supports or weakens the attribution hypothesis.","Deleted logs, timestamp manipulation, and unusual cleanup can be relevant, but ordinary privacy tools and system maintenance also produce similar-looking artifacts. Avoid treating every anomaly as deliberate anti-forensics. Preserve the original state, compare known-good behavior, and explain alternative causes."]}],"practice":{"title":"Build a local execution timeline","scenario":"A supplied fictional endpoint dataset contains a download, a process event, and an outbound connection.","steps":["Normalize timestamps and record clock uncertainty.","Link the download to the process through a stable artifact, not just filename similarity.","Compare the connection time and process relationship.","State what the sequence does and does not establish about user intent."],"debrief":"A timeline becomes persuasive when artifacts independently connect the same event sequence and limitations are explicit."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","mitre"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain storage and filesystem context and apply it to an investigation.","Explain memory and running state and apply it to an investigation.","Explain attribution and anti-forensics and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.8","prompt":"Why is a user-profile path alone weak proof of attribution?","options":["It contains no characters.","Accounts can be shared, compromised, or used by automated processes.","All filesystem paths are fabricated.","It proves physical presence in every case."],"answer":1,"explanation":"The profile identifies an account context; linking that context to a human requires corroboration.","unit":3}}}]]
